<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Woodes Rogers</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 1679–<a href="/years/1732">1732</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/18th-century">18th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Woodes Rogers was an English privateer and colonial governor who sailed round the world in 1708–1711, rescuing the castaway Alexander Selkirk on the way. Appointed first royal governor of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bahamas">Bahamas</a>, he arrived at Nassau in 1718 with a royal pardon, broke up the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/piracy">pirates</a>' republic and hanged those who returned to piracy. He served a second term from 1729 until his death in 1732.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/woodes-rogers.jpg" alt="Woodes Rogers" width="320" height="234" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: William Hogarth · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Rogers,Woodes.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Woodes Rogers was the eldest son of Woods Rogers, a successful merchant captain who held shares in many ships. He spent part of his childhood in Poole before the family moved to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bristol">Bristol</a>, and in November 1697 he was apprenticed to the Bristol mariner John Yeamans. He completed his apprenticeship in November 1704 and the following January married Sarah Whetstone, daughter of Rear Admiral Sir William Whetstone, and in 1706 his father died at sea, leaving him the family's ships and business. He also took part in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/atlantic-slave-trade">Atlantic slave trade</a>, and continued to do so for the rest of his life.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>During the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/war-of-the-spanish-succession">War of the Spanish Succession</a>, William Dampier proposed a privateering voyage against <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a>, and Rogers led it in the frigates Duke and Duchess, leaving <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bristol">Bristol</a> on 1 August 1708. He put down a mutiny, kept his crews from scurvy with limes, captured <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guayaquil">Guayaquil</a> for a modest ransom and took the Spanish ship Nuestra Señora de la Encarnación y Desengaño off <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mexico">Mexico</a>, being wounded in the face. His brother was killed in the Pacific. The investors roughly doubled their money, but Rogers was sued by over 200 of his crew and forced into bankruptcy. His book A Cruising Voyage Round the World sold well, partly because of Selkirk's story.</p><p>As governor of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bahamas">Bahamas</a>, Rogers arrived on 24 July 1718; the pirate <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/charles-vane">Charles Vane</a> escaped his trap, but Nassau and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-providence">New Providence</a> were secured. On 9 December 1718 he tried ten captured men, and eight were hanged. A Spanish attack in February 1720 withdrew in the face of two frigates and 500 militia. Unpaid for his outlay on defences, he sailed for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/britain">Britain</a> in March 1721, found a new governor appointed and was imprisoned for debt.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>Rogers helped the author of A General History of the Robberies and Murders of the Most Notorious Pyrates, which made him a national hero again. George I granted him a pension, and George II reappointed him governor. In his second term he dissolved the colonial assembly when it objected to a local tax. He died in Nassau on 15 July 1732. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/piracy">Piracy</a> expelled, commerce restored remained the motto of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bahamas">Bahamas</a> until independence in 1973.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Woodes Rogers” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The crown took direct control in 1718, when <b>Woodes Rogers</b> arrived as royal governor and suppressed the pirates; an elected House of Assembly followed in 1729.</blockquote><a href="/state/colony-of-the-bahamas">Colony of the Bahamas</a></li><li><blockquote>In 1718 <b>Woodes Rogers</b> arrived as royal governor with a royal pardon.</blockquote><a href="/state/republic-of-pirates">Republic of Pirates</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bristol">Bristol</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/united-kingdom">the United Kingdom</a>. Died in Nassau. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=51.45,-2.6&amp;year=1713" aria-label="Bristol on the atlas"><svg class="map map-globe map-loc" viewBox="0 0 10000 10000" role="img" aria-label="Globe: the birthplace of Woodes Rogers"><image href="/maps/globes/g000n60.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="10000"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M4862 5725h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M4862 5725h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=51.45,-2.6&amp;year=1713">What was here in 1713? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
